<%# Rights: the section with the most direct, measurable payoff, and the one
    whose honesty rule is strictest — it must never assert a jurisdiction's
    current law. The hedging discipline is a feature and is described as
    one. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    A great deal of what people are owed goes unclaimed for one reason:
    nobody sent the letter. The deposit isn't returned because no formal
    demand arrived. The insurance denial stands because it was never
    appealed. The debt collector keeps calling because nobody asked them to
    validate the debt inside the window where that works.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of these are hard letters to write. They're just letters most
    people have never seen, in a register most people have never had to
    write in, about a process nobody explained — and so they don't get
    written, and the money stays where it is.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What this section is</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    A way to get those letters written and sent, with the follow-through
    scheduled so a deadline doesn't quietly pass. You give a line of facts —
    moved out June 30th, £1,800 deposit, no itemised list yet — and get back
    a formal letter you can put your name to, plus the dates it implies on
    your calendar and the small next steps on your list.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("rights") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The situations it covers</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Renting") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Deposits not returned, repairs not made, notices that arrive looking
        more final than they are. Whether you own or rent is something the
        app knows, so the help you get is footed correctly.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Money owed and money demanded") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Wages you earned and weren't paid; collectors contacting you about a
        debt that may not even be yours. Both are time-boxed processes where
        acting inside the window is most of the game.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Being told no") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Insurance denials, benefit refusals, disputed bills. A large share of
        these are overturned on appeal, and a large share are never appealed.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Scary mail, in general") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The envelope you've been avoiding for a week. What it actually says,
        what really happens if you ignore it, and what to do next — which is
        often less alarming than the letter's tone implied.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Why it hedges, and why that's the point</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Deadlines and penalties are set by the law where you live, and those
    change. So the letters say that a deposit deadline is
    <em>commonly</em> a certain length and that you should verify the rules
    for your area — rather than stating a confident number that might be
    wrong, out of date, or from the wrong country.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    That restraint is deliberate. A letter citing a made-up statute is worse
    than no letter, because it hands the other side a reason to dismiss you.
    Everything here is written to be something you can send without
    checking whether it invented anything — because it didn't, and where it
    doesn't know a detail it leaves you a clearly marked blank rather than
    filling it in plausibly.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">It isn't a lawyer</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    It doesn't practise law, and it will not tell you whether you'd win.
    What it does is remove the two things that actually stop people: not
    knowing the letter exists, and not knowing how to start it. For a great
    many everyday disputes that turns out to be the whole obstacle.
  </p>
</section>
